Q: How to map numbered JSON fields to a list using Kotlinx Serialization A: <p>This is a classic example of a poorly designed Api. To handle this type of response i have 3 approaches.</p>
<ol>
<li>By defining a extention function on <code>JSONObject</code>.</li>
</ol>
<pre><code>fun JsonObject.extractNumberedPairs(keyPrefix: String, valuePrefix: String): List&lt;Pair&lt;String, String&gt;&gt; = 
keys.filter { it.startsWith(keyPrefix) }
.mapNotNull { key -&gt;
        // 2. Strip the prefix to get just the number → &quot;1&quot;, &quot;2&quot;, &quot;3&quot;...
        val index = key.removePrefix(keyPrefix)

        // 3. Get the ingredient name, skip if null or blank
        val keyVal = this[key]?.jsonPrimitive?.contentOrNull?.takeIf { it.isNotBlank() }

        // 4. Use that same index to look up the matching measure
        val valVal = this[&quot;$valuePrefix$index&quot;]  // → &quot;strMeasure1&quot;, &quot;strMeasure2&quot;...
            ?.jsonPrimitive?.contentOrNull
            ?.takeIf { it.isNotBlank() }

        // 5. mapNotNull: if ingredient is null, this pair is dropped entirely
        if (keyVal != null) keyVal to (valVal ?: &quot;&quot;) else null
    }
</code></pre>

Q: How to make a list of objects visible in Unity Inspector? A: <p>First of all I would recommend a <code>ScriptableObject</code> for this kind of profile things</p>
<pre><code>[CreateAssetMenu] // &lt;- can be further customized via parameters
public class WeaponProperties : ScriptableObject
{
    ...
}
</code></pre>
<p>This allows you to create assets via right click in the Project folder -&gt; Create -&gt; <code>WeaponProperties</code></p>
<p>Then in your class you simply expose a list/array</p>
<pre><code>[SerializeField] private WeaponProperties[] _availableWeapons;
</code></pre>
<p>and then you would simply go e.g. by index</p>
<pre><code>[SerializeField] private int _currentSelectedIndex;
</code></pre>
<p>and if you want can expose it directly as</p>
<pre><code>public WeaponProperties CurrentSelectedWeapon =&gt; _availableWeapons[_currentSelectedIndex];
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>If you rather want some meaningful fix weapon IDs you can indeed use an <code>enum</code>. In that case though I would recommend <a href="https://github.com/ayellowpaper/SerializedDictionary" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>SerializeDictionary</code></a> whdich would allow you to directly serialize a map <code>enum -&gt; WeaponProperties</code></p>
<pre><code>
public enum WeaponType

{      

    Pitola,

    Rifle

}

[SerializeField] private SerializedDictionary&lt;Weaponype, WeaponProperties&gt; _availableWeapons;

[SerializeField] private WeaponType _currentSelectedType;

public WeaponProperties CurrentSelectedWeapon =&gt; _avaialableWeapons[_currentSelectedType];
</code></pre>
